1. **An apparatus for managing access to a device across federated areas, the apparatus comprising:**  

   a memory configured to store:  
   (i) a base-area definition for a base federated area, the base-area definition specifying a base access policy and a base task routine;  
   (ii) a first-area definition for a first federated area, the first-area definition specifying a first access policy and a first task routine; and  
   (iii) a second-area definition for a second federated area, the second-area definition specifying a second access policy and a second task routine; and  

   one or more processors communicatively coupled to the memory and configured to:  

   establish an inheritance relationship in which the first federated area and the second federated area each inherit at least a portion of the base access policy from the base federated area;  

   receive a request to access the device, the request identifying a requesting entity and a requested task;  

   determine that the device or the requesting entity is associated with at least one of the first federated area or the second federated area;  

   generate an effective access policy for the request by combining the inherited portion of the base access policy with the access policy of each federated area associated with the device or the requesting entity, wherein an area-specific access-policy provision overrides a conflicting inherited base access-policy provision;  

   select, from among the base task routine, the first task routine, and the second task routine, a task routine applicable to the requested task according to a priority relationship under which (a) an applicable area-specific task routine has priority over the base task routine and (b) when both the first task routine and the second task routine are applicable, the task routine assigned a higher stored priority has priority over the other task routine; and  

   selectively authorize access to the device and cause the requested task to be performed using the selected task routine in response to determining that the request satisfies the effective access policy.
